
======================================================================================================================
PART I. THE GEOMETRY OF COMPARISON (mathematics; nothing is named physically here)
======================================================================================================================
ok   I.1 one system: four amplitudes, five Dirac matrices g_a g_b + g_b g_a = 2 delta_ab, Hermitian
ok   I.1 the five together are a number, g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 = +-1   [-1]
ok   I.1 sixteen observables 1 + 5 + 10, orthonormal
ok   I.1 the overlap of two sides: Q_n Q_m Q_n = ((1 + n.m)/2) Q_n
ok   I.2 two systems: the comparison L = 1(x)1 + sum g(x)g has spectrum 6^1, 2^10, (-2)^5   [[(np.float64(6.0), 1), (np.float64(2.0), 10), (np.float64(-2.0), 5)]]
ok   I.2 L = 2(P + E): P the exchange, E of rank one, E^2 = 4E, PE = EP = -E
ok   I.2 the matrix C of the quaternionic structure (C g^T C^-1 = g for every Dirac matrix) is unique up to a factor and antisymmetric
ok   I.2 one's own past and a neighbour: the quaternionic structure on one system exchanges P and E and keeps L
ok   I.3 the square root of the sign q_D = 1 - P_D + iP_D: q^2 = theta_D (the metric's sign), q^dagger q = 1
ok   I.3 i^|S| on the basis of the exterior algebra of the six directions: 1, i, -1, -i on 16, 12, 16, 20   [[16, 12, 16, 20]]
ok   I.4 four types d|6-d: rotations within / mixing the two sides 15/0, 10/5, 7/8, 6/9
ok   I.4 the null cone of a distinction of type 2|4, projectivized: on a complement of the radial null direction the metric has 3 + and 1 -: one time, three space   [(np.int64(3), np.int64(1), np.int64(0))]

======================================================================================================================
PART II. EXISTENCE: THE STEP B, THE LAW, AND EVERYTHING COUNTED ON B
======================================================================================================================
ok   II.1 P5: 80 directed edges (state, last change), 4 continuations, 320 transitions
ok   II.2 P4: the law det(I - uB) = (1 - u^2)^24 k0 k2^10 k4^5, k_N(u) = 1 + 4u^2 - u(5 - 2N)
ok   II.2 its leading zero: k0(1/4) = 0, every other factor nonzero on |u| <= 1/4
ok   II.3 the signed step (the Clifford sign of every transition): A_-^2 = 5I
ok   II.4 one step out and back to where it started: F(z) = z^4(1 + z)/(16 - 8z - 3z^2 - 3z^3); signed Phi(z) = -z^4/(16 + 3z^2)
ok   II.4 every first-return excursion closes, F(1) = 1, after F'(1) = 16 steps on average (the mean return time); Phi(1) = -1/19, Phi(i) = -1/13
ok   II.5 one excursion visits the symmetric states E n_F = 10 and the vector states E n_L = 5 times; Var n_F = 84, Var n_L = 12, Cov = 30, Var l = 156
ok   II.6 the available transitions: 74 directed edges, M = 296 transitions, C_h = 60 along h, 59 along each other direction, 34 edges whose state does not contain h
ok   II.7 the critical Green's function (5 - A)^+ = (7 + A)/32 - 3J/128; the effective resistance between the singlet and a symmetric state b = 7/16
ok   II.8 the symmetric-state visits of one excursion x(1 + x)/(16 - 11x - 3x^2), pole x_c = (sqrt 313 - 11)/6
ok   II.9 the polar step: singular values of B are 1 and 4, xi = log 2, tanh(xi/4) = tan^2(pi/8)
ok   II.10 one transition of the 320 marked with a phase: D_theta = D + (e^(-i theta) - 1) u D'/320

